m|key`. Most exact_text answers are plan values copied verbatim.\n15\t  From D8 there is also a **`v=3` plan**: it carries `role=` and `attack=` itself, and sits behind a `HUNTRESS_ANALYSIS_V1:<TAG>-` comment.\n16\t- The hard part is **naming CODE SHAPE**: control-flow, expansion, and function/invocation/property styles. These are GM synthetic labels that are not in the plan. Role and scenario are enums, so read `accepted_values`.\n17\t- Files are named by behaviour (host-context, memory-stage, embedded-write, remote-write, local-dispatch, staged-handoff/-chain, outbound-transfer, impact-plan, relay-handoff, schedule-descriptor). The answer-id is `<lang>-<role>-<field>`, and the artifact path picks the file. Don't trust the file name over the plan `steps`.\n18\t- The D8-D11 prompts are narrative (\"find the one that doesn't match its siblings\", \"expose the stage whose claimed family\n19\t  tree is impossible\", D10 \"name the subscription that would really survive a reboot\", D11 \"flag the call whose claimed\n20\t  purpose the evidence doesn't support\"). No question asked for that outlier/impossible stage. Answer the contract ids only.\n21\t- Time Trial windows are about 5-13 s (30 s on d1). Mastery has `setN.` ids: 2 sets on d2-d7, **5 sets on d8 (3075 ids, 22 s)\n22\t  and d9 (3475 ids, 30 s)**, 2 sets again on d10 (1506 ids, 32 s), d11 (1622 ids, 11 s), d12 (1710 ids, 30 s), d13 (1826 ids, 80 s) and d14 (1942 ids, 30 s); **5 sets again on d15 (5130 ids, untimed regular pool)**. The artifact was `evidence-0N.zip`, or (d6) `delivery N: evidence.zip`, or (d7-d9) ONE download\n23\t  `evidence-01.zip` that holds every `evidence-0N.zip`. Same-named deliveries: pair them by sha256. Timed question texts\n24\t  carry `[assigned-artifact-sha256:<hex>]` per member -- usable to verify the bytes you got.\n25\t\n26\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants of the SAME day -- parse every value listed)\n27\tEach miss below cost one failed timed attempt + a ~45-90 s generalize cycle (D6 replay: 4 cycles; D7: 1 cycle; D8-D14: 0;\n28\tD15: 1 TT localize + 1 Mastery cycle with 2 generalize workers, ~5 min, all from the GM-LABEL-REGIME axis below).\n29\t- **GM label regime per pool (D15, NEW; batch `expansion`)** -- SAME bytes, SAME question text, DIFFERENT label. Single solve\n30\t  (no `run_for_time` key) and every older/HoF timed case label the whole handoff: `echo(!var!)` => `delayed` unless a FOR/h-pool\n31\t  rule fires. The D15 untimed regular pool (contract `run_for_time:false`, `variant_fresh:true`; TT 0df5072d, Mastery 7fbfc526)\n32\t  labels the JOIN STATEMENT ALONE (54 GM labels, 0 conflicts): `!a!!b!` join = delayed; `for %%G in (..) do call set` = for_indirect\n33\t  for ANY pool (incl. `%%h%%G%%` with an `if defined` reset -- old regime said call_double); bare `call set \"x=%%h0%%..\"` (h pool)\n34\t  = call_double; bare `call set` over item/v_a/FrAg pools = percent; plain `set \"x=%a%%b%\"` = percent. The diff is in the\n35\t  contract's metadata, not the bytes -- two generalize workers burned time diffing byte-identical files. Parse rule: branch\n36\t  the label table on `contract.get(\"run_for_time\") is False`; otherwise keep the old regime. Expect other synthetic-label fields\n37\t  (control/style) to be able to flip the same way: when byte-identical constructs get different GM labels across cases,\n38\t  diff the CONTRACT keys (run_for_time, hall_of_fame_eligible, variant_fresh) first.\n39\t- **Codec per file** -- rep x transform x key re-rolls per variant; never bind a role to the sample's codec. **NEW rep\n40\t  NAMES appear only on variants** (D7 TT: `unicode_escapes` `\\u00XX..`, `json_numbers` `[232,241,..]`,\n41\t  `reversed_base64`, Bash `xor_hex|cd`). Decode by the material's SHAPE (escapes / int array / hex / base64, each also\n42\t  reversed) x the named transform, and accept only output starting `v=`; the name only orders the tries.\n43\t  D9: the 7 deliveries (single + TT + 5 sets) used no rep/transform beyond lib.codec's D3-D8 vocabulary (0 decode failures).\n44\t- **Codec spec arity** -- 3 fields `rep|transform|key`, 2 fields `rep|` (empty transform = identity; D7-D9 bash advanced\n45\t  fixtures), or the transform folded into the rep with the key second (`xor_hex|cd`, D8 `xor_hex|11`, D9 `xor_hex|95/6f/c0`).\n46\t  Split on `|`, never index blindly. lib.codec (`_spec`) normalises both short forms.\n47\t- **Plan carrier tag (v=3, D8-D9)** -- `HUNTRESS_ANALYSIS_V1:<TAG>-<data>`, plus the same data in `descriptor_<id> = \"..\"`.\n48\t  Values: `B64` (base64), `HEX` (plain hex), `HB64` (hex of base64 text = 2 layers), `FRAG` (hex split by `.` every 24 chars).\n49\t  Across all 7 deliveries of D8 and of D9 the tag stayed fixed per file; only the values re-rolled. Parse rule: ignore the tag\n50\t  name. Peel by shape and recurse (max 3 layers): if the text is only hex digits plus `.:,-_ |\\x%` separators, strip the\n51\t  separators and unhex; otherwise base64 (also reversed). Accept only a result starting `v=`. The tag = `obfuscation_profile`.\n52\t  `lib.codec.find_codecs(src)` returns the v=3 plan (tag -> rep: B64 base64_literal, HEX/FRAG hex_compact, HB64 hex_base64).\n53\t- **Plan carrier host syntax (v=3)** -- the marker sits in whatever comment the host uses: `//` (PHP, JS, HTA, WSF, SCT CDATA,\n54\t  MOF, TS), `--` (Lua D9, AppleScript D13), `;` (AutoIt, D12), `<!-- -->` (MSBuild XML, D14), `#` (Perl, D15), and the descriptor as `$descriptor_<id>`, `var descriptor_<id>`, `local\n55\t  descriptor_<id>`, `Global Const $descriptor_<id> = \"..\"` (AutoIt), `property descriptor_<id> : \"..\"` (AppleScript), an XML property element `<PropertyGroup><HuntressDescriptor>..</HuntressDescriptor>`\n56\t  (MSBuild, D14 -- no `descriptor_` name at all), `my $descriptor_<id> = \"..\";` (Perl, D15). Parse rule: regex\n57\t  `HUNTRESS_ANALYSIS_V\\d+\\s*:\\s*(\\w*)-?\\s*([^\\s\"'<>\\]]+)` over the raw bytes, independent of comment syntax and extension.\n58\t- **Numeric literal radix** -- the same array/constant may be decimal `23,7`, hex `0x17,0x07`, or a separator-joined\n59\t  string decoded with `parseInt(x, R)` / `int(x, R)`: READ THE RADIX FROM THE CODE, never guess it from the digits\n60\t  (D6: JScript material array hex on TT; Mastery dash-joined DECIMALS with `parseInt(..,10)`). Applies to keys too.\n61\t- **Base64 alphabet** -- standard and urlsafe (`-` `_`) both occur; strip/whitelist accordingly and pad (D6 Python TT).\n62\t- **Reversal level** -- `reversed_hex/_decimal/_integer_list` reverse the loaded LIST; `reversed_base64` reverses the\n63\t  TEXT before decoding (`==Adu..`). Try both orders.\n64\t- **Carrier of the same language** -- plain source vs a frozen executable (D6: Python plan inside a cx_Freeze build on\n65\t  a TT variant). Locate the material by its markers (`MATERIAL_`/`CODEC_`/`v=`/`HUNTRESS_ANALYSIS_`), not by file type.\n66\t- **Material layout** -- one literal, split chunks, `+=` concatenation, adjacent quoted strings, `.`-separated FRAG\n67\t  fragments; join before decoding.\n68\t- **Adapter values** re-roll within a role (Node stager http_then_module/https_then_vm/fetch_then_function; launcher\n69\t  execfile_node/fork_module/spawn_file; persistence systemd_descriptor/npm_lifecycle_descriptor) -- always plan-verbatim.\n70\t- **No-new-axis days**: D8 (TT 129bf46a, Mastery 07d5051a), D9 (TT dfcfb51e, Mastery 1dfd4cf6), D10, D11 (TT f99ea9ec,\n71\t  Mastery 2d722b36; the 4 TS files kept FRAG/HEX/B64/HB64 + adapters + next_language in every delivery) and D12 (TT d629142e,\n72\t  Mastery 635241de; the 4 `.au3` files the same), D13 (TT c739141b, Mastery 78b00cf3; 4 `.applescript` + 2 Bash), D14 (TT\n73\t  b7cd2e36, Mastery 8a650537; 4 `.proj` kept HB64/FRAG/HEX/B64 + steps + next_language in all 4 deliveries), D15 BYTES (TT 0df5072d,\n74\t  Mastery 7fbfc526: 4 `.pl` + perl_cgi host same shape in all 6 deliveries; only the label regime above changed): structure, tags,\n75\t  role/attack/steps/profiles identical to the single; only values re-rolled. All CORRECT first try.\n76\t\n77\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n78\t| You see | First move |\n79\t|---|---|\n80\t| `evidence.zip`, \"method=AES Encrypted\" | Already extracted by triage (`work/extracted/`, password `infected`) and every plan decoded in triage.md -- start from that, BUT diff the extracted member list against the contract's `artifact` names. **Triage silently skips each day's new extension**: D7 `.mjs`, D8 `.php` (HB64 peeled 1 layer only), D9 `.lua` (5 files, no decode lines). In solve.py open members with `lib.common.read_member` (Python zipfile cannot read AES). Mastery may be a zip inside a zip; walk it once. |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64eedee1-889a-4eb5-b636-2a20820e2d49","uuid":"4972bece-8996-4bfa-9c8c-b1b0e2b0ede2","timestamp":"2026-10-15T13:09:18.847Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/script-analysis.md","content":"# script-analysis — technique log (days 1-15; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (these are observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- There is one AES zip, `evidence.zip` (password `infected`), of inert \"Huntress Script Triage\" fixtures. **The contract is CUMULATIVE.** Each day keeps every earlier language's files and questions and adds ONE new host language or host format. The bytes and values re-roll every day:\n  PS (d1, 15 ids) -> +JScript (d2, 87) -> +Batch (d3, 159) -> +VBScript (d4, 231) -> +Python (d5, 357) -> +Bash (d6, 399)\n  -> +Node.js `.mjs` and 2 Bash advanced-syntax fixtures (d7, 469) -> +8 PHP \"polyglot\" files, 4 WSF/HTA/PHP-webshell \"host\"\n  files and 2 more Bash advanced fixtures (d8, 615) -> +4 Lua polyglot